### [13:13:xx UTC] Step 5 — Implement fix
Input: fat_llama/audio_fattener/feed.py's write_audio() and upscale().
Action: Rewrote write_audio() to accept `normalize=True` (default, backward compatible) and
`reference_amplitude=None`; FLAC keeps subtype 'PCM_24' (its real ceiling); WAV switches to
subtype 'DOUBLE' (64-bit float, Issue #18 item 1). When normalize=True, behavior is unchanged
(peak-normalize + clip). When normalize=False, divides by `reference_amplitude` (falling back to
peak if not given) instead of the buffer's own peak -- a lossless domain conversion, not a
loudness change -- and only clips for integer (non-float) subtypes, since DOUBLE stores any
value losslessly. Updated upscale()'s write_audio() call to pass
`normalize=toggle_normalize, reference_amplitude=audio.max_possible_amplitude` (Issue #18 item
2's wiring fix). Updated both functions' docstrings.
Reasoning: Directly implements the hypothesized fix; reuses the source's own known bit-depth
full-scale reference (pydub's AudioSegment.max_possible_amplitude) rather than inventing a new
concept.
Output: Source edited; two locations changed (write_audio signature/body, upscale's final
write_audio call + toggle_normalize docstring).

### [13:15:xx UTC] Step 6 — Analyze: first re-run (found a modeling error, revised)
Input: The two new tests from Step 4, now against the fixed source.
Action: Ran the tests. test_write_audio_normalize_false_preserves_relative_level passed.
test_write_audio_wav_uses_64bit_float_and_is_lossless FAILED: my first implementation had the
float/double-subtype + normalize=False branch skip scaling entirely ("no rescale needed"),
writing raw-PCM-scale values (~12345 peak) instead of the reference-amplitude-relative domain
(~0.38 peak) my own test expected.
Reasoning: On reflection, "preserve original signal levels" should mean the standard
int-PCM-to-float-PCM domain conversion (divide by the known bit-depth full-scale reference), not
"leave completely unscaled" -- the latter is technically lossless on disk but would play back at
an absurd, non-standard loudness (a naive player interpreting raw ~30000-scale float samples as
full-scale-normalized audio). This is exactly the "doesn't match -> revise hypothesis" branch of
the loop.
Output: Revised write_audio() so BOTH float and integer subtypes divide by
`reference_amplitude` (or fall back to peak) when normalize=False; only the subsequent clip is
now conditional on subtype (skipped for float/double, since they don't need it and clipping
there would needlessly discard genuine above-reference content). Re-ran both tests plus the
pre-existing test_write_audio: all three passed.

